        "pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln1179-1219"
      ],
      "reply": "The sentence is:\n\n“I—I’m a little girl,” said Alice, rather doubtfully, as she remembered\nthe number of changes she had gone through that day.\n\nWhat makes the moment especially awkward is that Alice knows what she means by “little girl,” but the situation—and the Pigeon’s label—makes her say it as though she has to argue herself back into the identity. The word is familiar, yet it no longer feels fully secure.\n\nThat may be part of what happens with “manager,” too. A new role can be real in practice before it feels natural in language. Saying it aloud can make you feel as if you’re claiming something that others might question, rather than naming something you’re already becoming. You might try treating the awkwardness not as evidence that the title is false, but as a sign that your sense of yourself is still catching up with your responsibilities. What part feels most awkward: the authority in the word, the expectations attached to it, or saying it about yourself?",
