    {
      "scene_id": "personal-reflection",
      "line_id": "personal-line",
      "input_line": "I still stumble over calling myself a manager. At today's team introduction I nearly said designer again, even though I like the new work. Could we think about why naming a new role feels so awkward, and find an introduction that feels honest to me?",
      "boundary_decision": "not_applicable",
      "routed_ceiling": null,
      "route_called": false,
      "boundary_support_memory_ids": [],
      "released_evidence_ids": [],
      "reply": "That awkwardness makes sense: a role can change before your self-description catches up. “Designer” may still feel like the truest shorthand for your history, while “manager” names work you’re growing into—not necessarily a complete replacement for who you are.\n\nYou could try an introduction that leaves room for both:\n\n- “Hi, I’m [name]. I’m a designer by background and now I manage the [team/project] team.”\n- “I’m [name], and I lead the [team] team. I came from a design background, so that still shapes how I work.”\n- “I’m [name]. I’m stepping into management for the [team], after working as a designer.”\n\nThe first is probably the most straightforward; the second may feel most honest if you want to keep continuity with your old role. You don’t have to make the new title feel emotionally settled before you say it. Which part feels more awkward: claiming authority, letting go of “designer,” or saying a title that still feels new?",
      "release_source": "muse_candidate",
      "provenance_verdicts": [
        "pass"
      ],
